WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.640
Container networking determines which services, networks,

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:06.580
and endpoints workloads can communicate with.

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:12.080
Exposed ports, service discovery, ingress, egress, API gateways,

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.100
and load balancers all shape the reachable surface.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:19.480
A secure design does not assume every container should talk to every other service.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.340
Network policies, at a high level,

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:27.600
help express allowed communication paths and reduce unnecessary lateral reach.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:31.100
Ingress and egress deserve equal attention.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.160
Ingress controls who can reach a service from outside or from other workloads.

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:38.020
Egress controls where a workload can send traffic.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:42.800
Egress is often overlooked, but it matters for data protection, dependency control,

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:44.620
and incident containment.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:49.080
A compromised or malfunctioning workload should not have unlimited network freedom by

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:50.180
default.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:56.640
Orchestrator access control is critical because orchestrators can deploy, modify, inspect,

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:59.820
connect to, and remove workloads.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:04.920
Service accounts, role-based access concepts, namespace or project boundaries,

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:07.620
and administrative access review all matter.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:12.800
A user or workload with broad orchestrator permissions may be able to read secrets,

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:17.900
change images, expose services, or observe sensitive application behavior.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:22.520
Admission policy concepts add another layer of control by checking deployment requests

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:24.100
before workloads run.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:29.360
Policies may require trusted registries, non-root users, resource limits,

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:32.780
approved image signatures, or restricted host mounts.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.560
The broader principle is governance: control who can deploy, what can be deployed,

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:42.100
how it can communicate, and who can observe or modify it after release.
